WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.300
It's hard to imagine a celebrated actor in a bad&nbsp;
film. Well, not only does it happen, but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.740
often than you would think, some of their worst&nbsp;
movies are released the same year as their best.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.980
After years of being a steady character actor,&nbsp;
Michael Keaton came back in a massive way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.320
2014. Headlining the Best Picture-winning&nbsp;
drama "Birdman" reaffirmed that Keaton not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.220
only had leading man chops but that&nbsp;
he could surprise people even decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.120
after his star-making performances in&nbsp;
"Mr. Mom," "Beetlejuice," and "Batman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.220
It was a revelatory turn that breathed&nbsp;
exciting life into Keaton's filmography.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.740
"Birdman” wasn't the only motion picture&nbsp;
he appeared in that year. 2014 also saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.920
the actor showing up in one of the&nbsp;
weakest titles in his filmography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.640
the instantly forgotten video&nbsp;
game adaptation "Need for Speed."

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.440
The misguided Aaron Paul vehicle saw&nbsp;
Keaton playing "Monarch," the host of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.600
an illicit racing competition. Keaton's work&nbsp;
in the movie sees him largely isolated from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.880
all the other prominent characters, speaking&nbsp;
expository dialogue into a microphone. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.220
it did give Keaton a chance to deliver some&nbsp;
amusingly over-the-top dialogue, the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.900
was otherwise appropriately forgettable&nbsp;
for such a disposable action feature.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.340
This was the total opposite of the bold&nbsp;
work in "Birdman" that would give Keaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.620
his creative renaissance. At least the dismal&nbsp;
quality of "Need for Speed" allowed audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.940
to further appreciate Keaton getting a&nbsp;
chance at a big comeback later that year.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:33.720
"Devil in a Blue Dress" wasn't a box office&nbsp;
monster when it first opened in theaters in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.300
but in the years since, audiences have grown&nbsp;
to love this Neo-noir anchored by Denzel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.820
Washington. It's hard not to be captivated by&nbsp;
Washington as detective Easy Rawlins. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.020
the visual palette of the feature is&nbsp;
gorgeous, not to mention impressive in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.880
how it juggles classic noir motifs without&nbsp;
sacrificing its own creative impulses.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.280
"Her name was Ruby Hanks&nbsp;
from Lake Charles, Louisiana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.600
And I suppose all she really&nbsp;
wanted was a place to fit in."

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.440
Even with Washington's impressive&nbsp;
resume, "Devil in a Blue Dress"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.240
stands alongside the likes of "Malcolm&nbsp;
X" as the man's greatest star vehicle.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.700
However, "Devil in a Blue Dress" wasn't the&nbsp;
only Denzel Washington film occupying movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.740
theaters in 1995. This same year saw&nbsp;
the debut of "Virtuosity" — an action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.900
thriller following Washington as a former cop&nbsp;
tasked with taking down a virtual criminal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.420
played by Russell Crowe, who's broken out into&nbsp;
reality. The feature was decimated by critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.100
and now ranks as one of the more puzzling&nbsp;
endeavors in Washington's filmography.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.280
After several years of being a notable supporting&nbsp;
character actor and occasionally headlining box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.660
office non-starters, Oscar Isaac made&nbsp;
the leap to successful leading man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.820
2013 with "Inside Llewyn Davis." Playing&nbsp;
the titular cash-strapped folk musician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.040
Isaac was a revelation. He delivered talented&nbsp;
performances before, but never to this degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:02.100
With so much screen time and a rich Coen Brothers&nbsp;
screenplay at his disposal, Isaac crafted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.540
character whose pain could simultaneously&nbsp;
irritate and devastate the audience.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.780
"Have you ever heard the&nbsp;
expression it takes two to tango?"

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.180
Isaac was also one of the leads of "In Secret"&nbsp;
— an erotic thriller from director Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.700
Stratton that began playing at film festivals&nbsp;
in 2013. Whereas "Inside Llewyn Davis" became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.980
a beloved masterpiece, "In Secret" was hated&nbsp;
by critics. Many of the biggest complaints were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.540
leveled at the feature's erratic tone&nbsp;
and its unappealing visual aesthetic.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.960
Though it had a one-week qualifying run&nbsp;
in 2014 to ensure it could compete in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.180
the 87th Academy Awards, the North American&nbsp;
theatrical release of "Still Alice" didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.180
begin in earnest until January 2015. Once this&nbsp;
film landed in theaters, audiences were quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:54.000
taken aback by Julianne Moore's staggering lead&nbsp;
performance as a woman dealing with Alzheimer's.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.800
It's a brutal arc to watch unfold, but&nbsp;
Moore handles all the finer details and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.720
quieter moments beautifully. In a career of&nbsp;
memorable go-for-broke performances in movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.940
like "Boogie Nights," her work in "Still Alice"&nbsp;
still registers as commanding and impressive.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.800
A few weeks after "Still Alice" began its&nbsp;
big-screen journey, another movie featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.260
Moore landed in theaters. This project was&nbsp;
"Seventh Son," a fantasy epic that saw Moore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.120
occupying the role of the film's villain. There&nbsp;
was no shortage of star power in "Seventh Son,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.580
including Jeff Bridges as a wise wizard, but it&nbsp;
wasn't enough to salvage an expensive disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.020
Not even Moore vamping it up as a baddie&nbsp;
was enough to make it a worthwhile watch.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.200
By 2009, Jeff Bridges had been scoring&nbsp;
high-profile roles in movies for nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:46.020
40 years. In that time, he'd explored countless&nbsp;
genres, established several iconic characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.720
and been nominated for four Academy Awards.&nbsp;
A staple of big-screen storytelling, Bridges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:57.480
was long-overdue for an Oscar. That's just what&nbsp;
happened when he played tormented country singer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:03.900
Otis "Bad" Blake in "Crazy Heart" in 2009. It's&nbsp;
a role that exploits Bridges' own legacy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.880
create a character whose aching weariness&nbsp;
seems so real. You believe Blake has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.920
through the fire and flames for decades. It's&nbsp;
a fantastic performance from Bridges, and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.480
that gave Hollywood and general moviegoers&nbsp;
alike a chance to appreciate his gifts.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.920
That same year, though, Bridges anchored&nbsp;
"The Open Road" with Justin Timberlake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.360
One would imagine that combining two&nbsp;
instantly recognizable names like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.320
would have resulted in something at least&nbsp;
interesting, but "The Open Road" became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.900
one of Jeff Bridges' most detested features.&nbsp;
The movie was plagued by rampant criticisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.460
suffering from weak writing and a lack of&nbsp;
interesting material for Bridges to handle.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.580
Looking to expand his image beyond just romantic&nbsp;
comedies like "Fool's Gold" and "How to Lose a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:53.340
Guy in 10 Days," Matthew McConaughey began to&nbsp;
tackle challenging roles and work with intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.680
auteurs in 2012. Embracing a wide variety&nbsp;
of projects meant that there were going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.800
inevitably be some duds. And such a dud came&nbsp;
in the form of "The Paperboy." A melodrama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.360
from director Lee Daniels, McConaughey was&nbsp;
one of the many actors in the ensemble cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.460
grappling with a thick accent and a plot&nbsp;
that could be borderline incomprehensible.

00:06:14.634 --> 00:06:18.594
"I'm the only one, I'm your mercy. I'm&nbsp;
your arms-wide-open, I'm your pants."

00:06:18.594 --> 00:06:22.980
"The Paperboy" has moments where it comes&nbsp;
to life, but it's mostly a wash thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:28.500
to lackluster style and extremely predictable&nbsp;
third-act narrative "twists." On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:34.260
McConaughey also managed to headline one of&nbsp;
his very best movies in 2012 by taking on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.780
supporting role in "Bernie." Playing an officer&nbsp;
who seems to be the only one suspicious of Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:44.280
Black's titular protagonist, McConaughey&nbsp;
portrays a unique character compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:49.020
his usual roles while simultaneously&nbsp;
demonstrating his deft comedic chops.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.800
It's hard to believe Gary Oldman didn't score an&nbsp;
Oscar nomination until 2011 with the espionage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:01.980
thriller "Tinker Tailor Soldier Spy." Regardless,&nbsp;
the nomination was well deserved. Oldman really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:06.960
excelled at communicating so much interior&nbsp;
life for his character within the intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.260
restrained ambiance of the feature. Famous for his&nbsp;
enjoyably over-the-top displays of acting, "Tinker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:18.720
Tailor Soldier Spy" reminded us all that Oldman&nbsp;
could do extraordinary subdued work as well.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.760
That same year, Oldman showed up as an&nbsp;
antagonistic figure armed with a gigantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.500
metal elephant in "Red Riding Hood." The&nbsp;
talented actor is no stranger to showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:34.260
up for supporting roles in schlocky genre fare,&nbsp;
but "Red Riding Hood" is one of the most tedious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.760
and forgettable movies in his career. A&nbsp;
paint-by-numbers attempt to make a moody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.560
"Twilight" knock-off with iconography lifted&nbsp;
from the "Little Red Riding Hood" fairy tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.460
"Red Riding Hood" is neither fun nor&nbsp;
trashy enough to be worth a watch.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:53.280
While some remember Robin Williams for his&nbsp;
rapid-fire wit and skillful impressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.980
an especially lasting quality of his career is&nbsp;
his gift for warmth. Williams could exude an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:04.500
inviting aura like nobody's business, and that&nbsp;
trait was particularly apparent in his role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.660
Dr. Sean Maguire in "Good Will Hunting."&nbsp;
In this film, Williams skillfully played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.520
man who could help withdrawn college students&nbsp;
come out of their shells while simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.320
conveying that his character was somebody&nbsp;
struggling to let go of his own past. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:24.840
was a complicated role, but one to which Williams&nbsp;
lent incredible authenticity and vulnerability.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:29.160
The same year Williams was crushing it in&nbsp;
"Good Will Hunting," he also anchored the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.380
comedy dud "Fathers' Day" with Billy Crystal.&nbsp;
One would imagine that pairing up Williams and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.760
Crystal would have inevitably resulted in&nbsp;
something memorable or amusing. But alas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.140
"Fathers' Day" is the worst kind of&nbsp;
comedy: utterly forgettable. Even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.520
Williams sometimes putting too much energy&nbsp;
into the film's big comedic set-pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.200
"Fathers' Day" was a snooze. While this feature&nbsp;
has faded away from the public consciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.700
films like "Good Will Hunting" exemplify&nbsp;
why Robin Williams won't be forgotten.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:03.720
The idea of Amy Adams playing Lois Lane sounds&nbsp;
like a great concept. Lois is a charming character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.940
who has endured in pop culture for a reason,&nbsp;
and the acting chops of Adams could lend her new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:14.760
levels of depth. Rather than being overwhelmed by&nbsp;
CGI mayhem or reduced to a cornball love interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:20.760
an actor of her caliber had the potential to stand&nbsp;
out and command the screen. 2013's "Man of Steel"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:26.340
introduced Adams' Lois, giving fans plenty of&nbsp;
reasons to look forward to more. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:32.700
the 2016 follow-up "Batman v Superman: Dawn of&nbsp;
Justice" offers Adams nothing of substance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:37.980
do. Her version of Lois Lane is saddled with a&nbsp;
dreary detective storyline that feels extraneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:43.020
from the central Batman/Superman plotline.&nbsp;
Worse, her exposition-heavy investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:47.520
scenes never give Adams a chance to indulge&nbsp;
in her greatest strengths as an artist.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.200
Thankfully, eight months after "Dawn of&nbsp;
Justice," audiences would see Adams in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.180
more substantive role as Louise Banks, the&nbsp;
linguist who functions as the protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.580
of "Arrival." Adams is endlessly compelling&nbsp;
in this part and provides a distinctly human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.560
anchor to orient a plotline involving human&nbsp;
beings trying to communicate with aliens.

00:10:07.756 --> 00:10:11.585
"I will take that as a compliment."
"Yeah well it is."

00:10:11.585 --> 00:10:17.880
Before he became Captain America, Chris Evans was&nbsp;
playing another Marvel Comics superhero: Johnny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:24.420
Storm, aka The Human Torch. His second outing as&nbsp;
the character came with "Fantastic Four: Rise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:29.580
the Silver Surfer," which attempted to work more&nbsp;
of the weird cosmic elements from Fantastic Four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:35.460
comics into the big screen version of Marvel's&nbsp;
first family. Unfortunately, these ambitions just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:41.400
resulted in an even more underwhelming movie&nbsp;
than the already subpar 2005 "Fantastic Four"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:46.920
feature. The interpretations of famous comic&nbsp;
book characters like Galactus in "Rise of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.360
Silver Surfer" were terrible, and the film's&nbsp;
broad attempts at comedy weren't much better.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.480
That same year, Evans also headlined one of the&nbsp;
very best works in his filmography: "Sunshine." A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:03.900
Danny Boyle directorial effort anchored by Cillian&nbsp;
Murphy, Evans got far meatier and atmospherically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:09.360
interesting material to work with in "Sunshine."&nbsp;
Not only that, but the project demonstrated his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.100
gift for working well within ensemble casts —&nbsp;
a trait that would suit him perfectly in later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.780
motion pictures like "Snowpiercer," "Knives Out,"&nbsp;
and even his final two appearances as Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:24.120
America. "Rise of the Silver Surfer" proved that&nbsp;
not even the charm of Chris Evans could salvage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:30.180
this ill-conceived vision of a superhero team. By&nbsp;
contrast, "Sunshine" offered a glimpse into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.280
kind of talents Evans can express when he's given&nbsp;
the room and some quality writing to work with.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:42.600
In 2003, "Tiptoes" debuted on the American film&nbsp;
festival scene, inspiring moviegoers everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.340
to ask where Matthew McConaughey and Gary&nbsp;
Oldman were going in their leading man careers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.440
But Oldman and McConaughey weren't the only ones&nbsp;
slumming it in this miscalculated motion picture;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:58.380
Peter Dinklage was also in the cast of "Tiptoes."&nbsp;
Sadly, not even the versatility and exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:03.360
talents of Dinklage were enough to lift this film&nbsp;
out of the gutter. Decades after its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.760
"Tiptoes" remains a pop culture punching bag&nbsp;
and a low point for many of the actors involved.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:13.920
That same year, Dinklage anchored the&nbsp;
easygoing and quietly moving indie feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.180
"The Station Agent." A movie hailing&nbsp;
from "Spotlight" director Tom McCarthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:23.220
"The Station Agent" gave Dinklage ample room&nbsp;
to play a fascinating protagonist with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:27.900
incredibly gruff exterior hiding complexities&nbsp;
that ensure you can't turn away from him.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.280
"You don't really say much do you?"&nbsp;

00:12:30.165 --> 00:12:31.868
"Guess not."
"Guess not."

00:12:31.868 --> 00:12:37.980
Needless to say, Dinklage is remarkable in "The&nbsp;
Station Agent," establishing the kind of quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:43.200
performances audiences would see him deliver&nbsp;
countless times over the years to come. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:48.780
"The Station Agent" garnered Dinklage considerable&nbsp;
awards attention, it'd be easy to forget that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:54.180
the same year that this film was released, he was&nbsp;
connected to a movie as despised as "Tiptoes."

